<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kurume</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kurume is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka-prefecture">Fukuoka Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, on the Chikugo river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>. From 1621 to 1871 it was the castle town of the Kurume domain, ruled by the Arima clan. It is known for Kurume kasuri, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>-dyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth, for the head shrine of the Suitengu cult, and as the birthplace of the Bridgestone tyre company.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurume.jpg" alt="Kurume" width="320" height="539" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tatawidepine · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurume_montage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kurume lies on the Chikugo Plain in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka-prefecture">Fukuoka Prefecture</a>, about 40 kilometres from the prefectural capital at Fukuoka City. The Chikugo River flows from the north-east to the south-west of the city and marks much of its boundary, and the Minou Mountain Range, with Mount Takatori, Mount Hatsushin and Mount Mino, rises along its southern and south-eastern side. Its climate is humid subtropical, with the most rain in July. In 2005 the surrounding towns of Kitano, Jōjima, Mizuma and Tanushimaru were merged into it, giving the city a long east-west shape.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was part of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikugo-province">Chikugo Province</a> and was the site of its provincial capital, its kokubun-ji and its ichinomiya. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> it came under the Kurume Domain, ruled for most of its history by the Arima clan, whose policy of promoting industry developed the castle town around Kurume Castle into a commercial centre. The clan's family temple, Bairin-ji, was founded near the castle. The modern city was established on 1 May 1889, with the creation of the modern system of municipalities.</p><p>From 1915 to 1919 over 1,000 German and about 50 Austro-Hungarian prisoners of war, taken after the surrender at Tsingtao, were held in an internment camp at Kurume, the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Exchanges between the prisoners and local people left a permanent mark on the city, and the prisoners' work and skills contributed to the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> industry that became the basis of the tyre maker Bridgestone. Kurume was a garrison town for the Imperial Japanese Army and from 1922 a centre of the rubber and chemical industries. On 11 August 1945 an air raid by 53 B-24 bombers killed 212 people and destroyed much of the urban centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kurume is a regional commercial centre. Bridgestone was founded here by Shōjirō Ishibashi (1889–1976), whose family made jika-tabi, the sock-like shoes of farmers, and found that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> soles protected the wearers from parasitic worms in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> paddies; the company's original plant remains in the city. Traditional products include kasuri, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>-dyed woven cloth, and rantai shikki, lacquered bamboo trays and bowls. The city has many National Historic Sites, among them Kofun burial mounds and the Chikugo Kokufu ruins, and attractions such as Kurume Castle, Kōra taisha, Kurume Suitengū and Zendō-ji, the head temple of the Jōdo sect in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kurume” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Tanaka family who held Chikugo died out, the shogunate divided the province, and in 1621 Arima Toyouji, who had fought for Tokugawa Ieyasu, received <b>Kurume</b> with about 210,000 koku.</blockquote><a href="/state/kurume-domain">Kurume Domain</a></li></ul></section>
